{
  "title": "Tune - Use RFT to Move to a Smaller Model",
  "locked": true,
  "description": "Shows how reinforcement fine-tuning in Azure AI Foundry can improve agent decisions enough to shift work from a larger model to a smaller one. Walks through the reward function, scenario-based training data, and evaluation results that demonstrate whether the tuned smaller model preserves quality while reducing cost.",
  "rows": [
    {
      "locked": true,
      "locks": {
        "time": true,
        "narrative": true,
        "demo_actions": true,
        "screenshot": true,
        "visual": true,
        "design_plan": true
      },
      "time": "~20s",
      "narrative": "- Up to this point, we have a working agent.\n- But it depends on a larger model to make the right calls.\n- The goal here is to keep the decision quality and lower the cost.\n- This is where reinforcement fine-tuning comes in.",
      "demo_actions": "Frame the current architecture in Foundry as a working agent that currently relies on a larger model for decision quality. Set up this chapter as a cost reduction story: can we train a smaller model to make the same decisions well enough to take over more of the work?",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_081728_325_42.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": true,
      "locks": {
        "time": true,
        "narrative": true,
        "demo_actions": true,
        "screenshot": true,
        "visual": true,
        "design_plan": true
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "- Before we tune anything, it helps to place RFT in the bigger fine-tuning picture.\n- Foundry supports SFT, DPO, and RFT.\n- Microsoft says most teams should start with SFT.\n- But when the task is about making the right decision, and we can score that clearly, RFT is a strong fit.",
      "demo_actions": "Open the Foundry fine-tuning overview. Highlight SFT, DPO, and RFT. Explain that this use case is about decision quality on a measurable task, which makes RFT a good method for training a smaller specialist model.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_012438_tuning-options.png"
    },
    {
      "locked": true,
      "locks": {
        "time": true,
        "narrative": true,
        "demo_actions": true,
        "screenshot": true,
        "visual": true,
        "design_plan": true
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "- This is the reward function step.\n- This is where we define what a good decision looks like in code.\n- The model is not being rewarded for sounding polished.\n- It is being rewarded for choosing the correct action.",
      "demo_actions": "Open the repo file for the reward or grader logic, such as rl/retail_grader_rft_tools.py. Introduce it as the mechanism that scores whether the smaller model is making the right business decisions.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_081852_660_50.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": true,
      "locks": {
        "time": true,
        "narrative": true,
        "demo_actions": true,
        "screenshot": true,
        "visual": true,
        "design_plan": true
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "- In this repo, the grader acts like an automated judge.\n- It checks whether the agent picked the right decision, used the right tools, and followed the right order.\n- If there is a refund or credit involved, it can also verify the exact amount.\n- That gives us a concrete target for training.",
      "demo_actions": "Scroll through the grader file and point out the business criteria it scores: correct decision, correct amount, correct tools, and correct workflow. Explain that this creates a measurable standard the smaller model can be trained against.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_081852_660_50.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": true,
      "locks": {
        "time": true,
        "narrative": true,
        "demo_actions": true,
        "screenshot": true,
        "visual": true,
        "design_plan": true
      },
      "time": "~25s",
      "narrative": "- Next is the training data.\n- These JSONL rows are not generic conversations.\n- They are work scenarios that show the model what kinds of requests it needs to handle.\n- This is how we teach the smaller model the operating patterns of the business.",
      "demo_actions": "Open the data JSONL files. Show that each record includes the user request, policy or system context, expected resolution, expected tools, expected actions, expected amounts, and scenario metadata.",
      "screenshot": ".cutready/screenshots/20260630_082004_088_54.jpg"
    },
    {
      "locked": true,
      "locks": {
        "time": true,
        "narrative": true,
        "demo_actions": true,
        "screenshot": true,
        "visual": true,
        "design_plan": true
      },
      "time": "~25s",
      "narrative": "- Then we test whether the tradeoff works.\n- The leaderboard compares the larger model, the smaller base model, and the tuned smaller model on the same held-out set.\n- If the tuned model closes the quality gap enough, we can shift work to it and spend less.\n- This is the proof point for the whole exercise.",
      "demo_actions": "Show the console, eval output, or leaderboard. Compare a larger frontier model, a smaller untuned base model, and the fine-tuned smaller specialist on the same held-out set. Call out quality, tool use, task success, and cost.",
      "screenshot": null,
